<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saltés</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saltes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saltés is an island in the marshes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odiel">Odiel</a> estuary opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In the Islamic period it held the town of Shaltish, a seat of the Banu Bakr's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> in the 11th century and a centre of iron working whose products were exported along the Atlantic coast. The town declined after the Christian conquest in the 13th century, and its remains have been excavated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saltes.jpg" alt="Saltés" width="320" height="266" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: grabado siglo XVIII · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MapatoograficoHuelvasiglo18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saltés is the southernmost island of the Odiel Marshes Natural Park, the second-largest wetland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, and lies at the meeting of the Odiel and Rio Tinto rivers; it forms part of the Odiel Marshes biosphere reserve. It is mostly swamp, with sand-covered ridges up to 3 metres high, and is home to birds such as spoonbills, flamingos, egrets and storks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The island and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a> are among the possible sites of the semi-mythical city of Tartessos, which the Roman poet Avienius, in his Ora Maritima, called the island between two rivers. Excavations have found Roman installations for fishing and the making of sea salt, and the remains of the eleventh-century Muslim town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of Saltés and Huelva. It had a central fortress of about 70 by 40 metres, and its people lived from metalworking, using ores brought down the Rio Tinto from the northern mines, as well as from fishing and trade by sea.</p><p>On 17 July 1381, in the Third Ferdinand War, the Battle of Saltés Island was fought nearby. The Castilian admiral Fernando Sánchez de Tovar, outnumbered by the Portuguese fleet of João Afonso Teles de Menezes, retreated towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. When the pursuing Portuguese broke formation and some of their ships attacked the fishermen's property on the island, the Castilians turned on them in tight formation and captured 22 of the 23 Portuguese galleys.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> the Francoist side set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> on the island for Republican prisoners. Its official capacity was under 2,000, but by March 1939 it held 3,197 prisoners in awful conditions, and oral testimonies put the number of inmates at more than 5,000. The camp was open at least from February to September 1939.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saltés Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1012, during the collapse of caliphal power, the Arab family of the Banu Bakr made themselves independent in Huelva and the island town of <b>Saltés</b> at the mouth of the Odiel and Tinto rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-huelva">Taifa of Huelva</a></li></ul></section>
